-- title:
--   Non-degeneracy of divisorial Weil pairings on Pic⁰[n]
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$ and let $F$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, subject to the hypothesis `hfg` that some $x \in F$ is transcendental over $K$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15), that is: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor, namely a finitely supported function $D$ on the places of $F/K$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing $K$, distinct from $F$, and principal ideal rings) with $D(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ for all $v$ and $\deg D = 0$; each place has residue field finite-dimensional over $K$; and $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank one over $F$. Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number and let $e$ be a divisorial Weil pairing datum of level $n$, i.e. a map `pair` from pairs of elements of the $n$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)$ (degree-zero divisors modulo principal divisors) to $K$ such that for every Weil datum $d$ of level $n$ one has $\mathrm{pair}(\mathrm{classLeft}\,d, \mathrm{classRight}\,d) = d.\mathrm{pairing}$, together with a moving property: each $n$-torsion class is represented by a degree-zero divisor supported at rational places avoiding any prescribed finite set of places. The conclusion is that the additive homomorphism `e.toHom`, sending an $n$-torsion class $x$ to the $K$-valued additive character $y \mapsto \mathrm{pair}(x,y)$ on the $n$-torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, is injective.
--
--   This is non-degeneracy of the Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion of the Jacobian of a curve over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, expressed in the divisorial language of degree-zero divisor classes. It is the form of non-degeneracy used on the modular-curve side, where it is invoked for annihilator computations on torsion points of $J_H$ and for vanishing statements about Tate modules.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_DivisorialWeilPairingData_toHom_injective_of_isCurveOver.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver
